.TH QADAM-FLOW-WORKER 1 "October 2026" "Qadam Flow" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qadam-flow-worker \- launcher for the Qadam Flow flow-execution worker process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qadam-flow-worker
[\fB\-\-\fR \fInode-arguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qadam-flow-worker
starts a Qadam Flow execution worker (the \fBworker\fR containers of the
Docker image). It is meant to be run by
.BR systemd (1)
as \fBqadam-flow-worker.service\fR, not by hand.
.PP
Configuration is read from
.B /etc/qadam-flow/worker.conf
(systemd \fIEnvironmentFile\fR). A worker is stateless: it polls the master
over HTTP (\fBAP_WORKER_API_URL\fR) and authenticates with the minted
\fBAP_WORKER_TOKEN\fR; it deliberately does \fBnot\fR talk to PostgreSQL or
Redis, which is what makes it installable on separate hosts. A small health
server listens on
.B AP_PORT
(default 3001) on localhost, a
.B qadam-flow-worker@N.service
style second instance needs an overridden port (systemctl edit).
.PP
Worker concurrency is \fBAP_WORKER_CONCURRENCY\fR (default 5, the Docker
compose replica count). Execution modes: \fBUNSANDBOXED\fR /
\fBSANDBOX_CODE_ONLY\fR by default; \fBSANDBOX_PROCESS\fR additionally uses
the isolate sandbox (see README.Debian for its runtime privileges).
.PP
Any extra arguments are passed to
.B node bootstrap.js
after the
.B \-\-
separator.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/qadam-flow/worker.conf
Conffile (0600). \fBAP_WORKER_TOKEN\fR is minted automatically when the
master is co-installed, otherwise see README.Debian.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qadam-flow-master (1),
.BR qadam-flow-mint-worker-token (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
.PP
Install, distributed-worker and sandboxing notes:
.B /usr/share/doc/qadam-flow-common/README.Debian
and https://flow.aiqadam.org/docs
